## Java Code Run Hone Ka Poora Safar

Tune bilkul sahi samjha — `.java` file seedha nahi chalti। Uska ek **journey** hota hai:

```
Step 1: Tu code likhta hai
────────────────────────
HelloWorld.java  ← ye teri file

Step 2: Compiler (javac) kaam karta hai
────────────────────────────────────────
HelloWorld.java
      ↓
  [javac]
      ↓
HelloWorld.class  ← ByteCode bana! (Platform independent)

Step 3: JVM kaam karta hai
───────────────────────────
HelloWorld.class (ByteCode)
      ↓
  [JVM — Interpreter + JIT]
      ↓
Machine Code ← Ab ye specific platform ke liye hai
      ↓
Output: "Hello World" screen par!
```

---

## Compiler vs Interpreter — Dono Kyun?

Ye important question hai — Java mein **dono** hain। Kyun?

### Compiler (javac)
- `.java` → `.class` (ByteCode) banata hai
- **Ek baar** poora code translate karta hai
- Errors pehle pakad leta hai — run karne se pehle

### Interpreter (JVM ke andar)
- ByteCode ko **line by line** machine code mein convert karta hai
- Platform specific hota hai
- **JIT (Just In Time) Compiler** bhi hota hai JVM mein — frequently use hone wale code ko fast banata hai

> **Simple analogy:** Compiler = poori book ek baar translate karo। Interpreter = ek ek line padh ke sunao।
> Java dono ka **best of both worlds** use karta hai! 🔥

